{"duration_api_ms":8585,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"be267004-232f-4e28-91c7-9059478831c6","total_cost_usd":0.0009764000000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1394,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":986},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1394,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":1255,"outputTokens":1417,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":712,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0009764000000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":986,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":712,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Why the ETF can distribute fewer gains:** Under IRC §852(b)(6), a RIC recognizes no gain when it distributes appreciated securities in redemption of its shares. ETFs use this routinely. Authorized participants redeem ETF shares for a basket of securities, and the fund can choose low-basis lots to hand out, purging embedded gains without a taxable sale. A cash-dealing mutual fund must sell holdings to meet redemptions, realizing gains that flow to all remaining shareholders.\n\n**Limits of the benefit:**\n- It covers only in-kind redemptions. Cash creations or redemptions, and cash-in-lieu components, force taxable sales.\n- Sales for rebalancing, index reconstitution, dividend or corporate-action handling, or portfolio changes still generate gains that must be distributed.\n- Fund-level gains are not eliminated; §852 still requires distributing at least 90% of income, and the 4% excise tax applies.\n- It defers rather than erases tax. Investors owe tax on their own gains when they sell shares.\n- Benefits depend on the fund's shareholder flows and basket design. Less-liquid or non-US holdings often rely on cash, reducing the advantage.","ttft_ms":5996,"type":"result","duration_ms":8011,"uuid":"2aa3f180-8c69-420b-bbab-d2e73c811c76","ttft_stream_ms":459,"time_to_request_ms":90,"first_content_frame_ms":459,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
